  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday night through Monday: A slow-moving system will crawl\nacross the Inland Northwest, allowing colder air to seep into \nthe region. With the system will come light to moderate rain, \nlight mountain snow, and breezy winds out of the south.\n\nPrecipitation amounts with this system have trended higher in\nlatest model runs. Most locations across North ID and the \neastern third of WA can expect between 0.25 and 0.50 inches of \nrain with a 20-40% chance for receiving more than 0.50 inches. \nLower amounts are expected in the lee of the Cascades and into \nthe western Columbia Basin where precipitation amounts will \ngenerally be between 0.10 and 0.25 inches. \n\nAs for the mountains, Washington Pass will carry the highest \nprobability for several inches of snow with the forecast \ncalling for 2-4 inches. There is a 30 percent chance of more\nthan 4 inches of snow by Monday evening. Other mountain passes \nincluding Lookout and Stevens will have lower chances for \naccumulating snow with precipitation transitioning back and \nforth between rain and snow. Rain and areas of fog will be the \nmain conditions impacting travel across the lowlands. \n\nFor winds, expect breezy southerly winds across the Columbia \nBasin and up the Okanogan Valley with sustained speeds of 15-20\nmph and gusts up to 30 mph Sunday afternoon.\n\nTuesday through Wednesday morning: Conditions look dry for this\nperiod with the region in between systems. Temperatures will be\ncooler than what we've been seeing with afternoon highs in the\nlow to mid 40s and overnight/early morning lows in the 20s to\nlow 30s. \n\nWednesday through Thursday: After a brief break from \nprecipitation, another system will approach the West Coast \nWednesday evening, bringing additional chances for rain and \nsnow. Ensemble members are coming into better agreement on the \ntrack of this system, with nearly all members showing the low \ndiving south of the region, moving inland between southern OR \nand central CA. The southerly track of the low will keep the \nbulk of the moisture and dynamics out of our area, but we will \nstill see a limited amount of moisture wrap around the low, \nbringing potential for light rain and/or a rain/snow mix to the \neast slopes of the Cascades and Okanogan highlands. Snow levels\nwill range from 2.5 kft to 3.5 kft Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nFriday onward: There is decent agreement on high pressure\nreturning to the Northwest, bringing another period of light \nwinds and dry conditions. There is a strong likelihood that this\npattern will promote expansive fog and low clouds following the \nprecipitation events this week. Strong surface inversions and\npoor mixing will also likely keep temperatures cool with highs\nin the 30s to lower 40s. /Fewkes",
